2. MPL-2.0 — file-level copyleft, notice and source availability
----------------------------------------------------------------

MPL-2.0 is a file-level copyleft licence: using an MPL-covered file obliges you
to keep that file under MPL-2.0, and distributing it obliges you to make the
Corresponding Source of those files available under MPL-2.0. It does **not**
obligate the whole binary to become MPL-2.0.

The following crates, in their published versions, are licensed MPL-2.0 and are
in the runtime dependency graph of the released binary (measured with
`cargo tree -p webfang_cli --features "ai mcp" -e normal`, which is the feature
set the release pipeline builds):

    cssparser 0.36.0
    cssparser 0.37.0
    dtoa-short 0.3.5
    option-ext 0.2.0
    selectors 0.35.0
    selectors 0.37.0
    selectors 0.38.0

webfang does not modify these crates. They are consumed as published, and the
exact versions above are pinned in `Cargo.lock`, which is part of the source
release for every tag. Corresponding Source for these files is therefore
available as the upstream crates.io release of each exact version and as the
source archive of the corresponding webfang tag.

A redistributor who ships a modified build of any of these files takes on the
MPL-2.0 §3.2 obligations for those files directly. Shipping the unmodified
binaries does not extend the copyleft beyond the files it always covered.


3. LGPL-3.0 — no obligation arises
-----------------------------------

No crate in either lockfile is licensed solely under the LGPL, and no LGPL
obligation arises for the shipped binaries.

Two points of record, because the `[licenses] allow` list in `deny.toml` reads
as if it did:

  * That allow list contains the entry `"LGPL-3.0",  # wreq-util (TLS
    emulation)`. The inline justification is **stale**: `wreq-util 3.0.0-rc.14`
    is licensed `Apache-2.0`
    (`https://github.com/0x676e67/wreq-util`), measured from `cargo metadata`.
    Keeping the entry is harmless — an allow-list entry is a permission, not a
    claim — but the comment attributing it to `wreq-util` is wrong and is
    reported for correction rather than silently edited here, because
    `deny.toml` is owned by a different work unit of issue #1607.
  * The full locked graph does contain `r-efi 5.3.0` and `r-efi 6.0.0`,
    licensed `MIT OR Apache-2.0 OR LGPL-2.1-or-later`. They are tri-licensed
    with a permissive option, which is the option in use, and neither version is
    in the released binary's runtime graph.

If a future dependency introduces a genuinely LGPL-licensed component, the
static-linking obligation (provide the library's own source, or relink against
a modified version) has to be met here rather than waved at.


4. OpenSSL and BoringSSL — statically linked
--------------------------------------------

The HTTP client chain is `wreq` → `btls` → `btls-sys`, and `btls-sys` compiles
**BoringSSL from C++ source** and links it **statically** into the shipped
binaries. This is why building from source requires `cmake` (BoringSSL's build
needs it) and why the Linux artifacts link glibc directly.

  * `btls-sys 0.5.6` — the build shim; declared licence `MIT`.
  * `btls 0.5.6`     — the Rust binding; declared licence `Apache-2.0`.

BoringSSL itself is distributed by the BoringSSL project under an ISC-style
licence **and** under the OpenSSL/Apache-2.0 dual terms. The authoritative text
is the project's own `LICENSE` file at
<https://github.com/google/boringssl>; it is reproduced in the
`THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.txt` bundle that ships with each release, together with the
attribution the chosen terms require.

**No advertising clause applies.** The obligation to display an "advertising"
acknowledgement belongs to the older 4-clause BSD / OpenSSL licence variant, not
to the ISC or the Apache-2.0 option. webfang is built against BoringSSL's
Apache-2.0 / ISC terms, and this file asserts no advertising obligation. This is
a positive control: a future edit that adds an advertising clause here would be
claiming a restriction the licences used do not impose.

Related, and also statically linked: `ring 0.17.14` (`Apache-2.0 AND ISC`).
`openssl` / `openssl-sys` appear in the *build* graph of the workspace but not in
the released binary's runtime graph, so no OpenSSL-library obligation attaches to
the shipped binaries today.


5. Other permissive licences in the runtime graph
--------------------------------------------------

The runtime graph is otherwise permissive. `Zlib`-licensed components (`foldhash`
0.1.5 and 0.2.0, and several `Zlib OR Apache-2.0 OR MIT` crates) require the
Zlib acknowledgement to travel with the binary; it is in the notices bundle.
`ISC`-licensed crates (`ego-tree`, `rustls-webpki`, `scraper`, `untrusted`, and
`ring` above) require their copyright notice and permission notice to be
retained; they are in the notices bundle.

`self_cell 1.3.0` is licensed `Apache-2.0 OR GPL-2.0-only`; the Apache-2.0
option is the one in use, so no GPL obligation attaches.

No SSPL, AGPL, or standalone-GPL-only licence expression exists anywhere in
either lockfile. `deny.toml`'s `[licenses] allow` list is the enforced statement
of that, and `cargo deny check licenses` runs on every pull request.


6. Reproducing the machine-readable forms
------------------------------------------

    bash scripts/generate-sbom.sh out.spdx.json
    bash scripts/generate-third-party-notices.sh out.txt

Both are pure functions of the commit: no timestamp, byte-collation sorting, no
floating tool download, no new dependency, and `--locked` on every cargo
invocation. Re-running either over the same commit produces byte-identical
output, which is what makes it meaningful to sign the result.
